# Chapter 10, part 2: dictionaries, messages, examples.
.h2 Word lists and dictionaries [convert-dict]
.ix Dictionaries, conversion
.ix Personal word list
.ix Word list, from a document
.ix Word list, as a document
The words that the user adds to the spell check are a text file, the personal
word list, in the directory of the settings ({c:~/.strata/dictionary}), so the work on a list of words is done
with text, as the table says.  The checks of the spelling are made by a
program of the machine, and its own dictionaries are not changed by Strata.
.table 24 36
To | What to do
Make a list of words from a document | Write the words one to a block in a
 document, and export it with Export Data Only; the file has one word on
 each line.  Copy the file to the personal word list, which is plain text
 in UTF-8.
Add the words of a document to the list | Import that file with
 Maintain Dictionaries ({n:str-spell-words-import}), or add its lines to
 the end of the personal word list.
Make a document of the list | Import the personal word list with Preserve
 Line Endings and the Character Set UTF-8; each word is a line.  A letter
 of the list that the document cannot show becomes a question mark.
Remove a word | Use Maintain Dictionaries ({n:str-spell-words}), or delete its
 line from the file.
.end
.caution The personal word list belongs to the user that owns the settings
  directory.  A file that is not owned by the user, or a directory that is
  not, is not used ({n:appcfg}).  Copy the file with the mode of an
  ordinary file of your own; the program makes it private by itself.
.h2 Messages of the conversions [convert-messages]
.ix Messages, Import and Export
.ix Conversion messages
These are the messages that the conversions of this chapter can show, with the
place where they appear.  The messages of the panels are listed again in
{x:file-messages} and those of the Printer in {n:appcmd}.  A message is
given as it is written; the part in capitals stands for the name of a file.
.table 26 34
Message | Meaning and what to do
No file name specified | The field ASCII File Name of Import or Export
 is empty.  Type a name; the panel stays open.
Import failed: cannot open file | The file does not exist or cannot be
 read.  The name is looked for in the Working Directory unless it is a path.
Import failed: this is a directory, not a file | The name is the name of a
 directory.  Type the name of a file.
Import failed: read error | The file was read only in part.  Try again; look
 at the disk.
Imported FILE | The text of the file is in the document at the cursor.
Imported; N characters not kept | The same, for UTF-8 or Latin-1: N characters
 of the file have no place in the document and are question marks.
Overwrite old version on disk? | Export: the file exists; Yes replaces it.
Export failed: cannot write FILE | The directory does not exist or is not
 yours, or the disk is full.
Export failed: write error | The text was not written completely.
Exported FILE | The file was written.
Retrieve failed: The file is not a document of this software. | Retrieve was
 given a file that is not a document, such as a text file.  Use Import for
 text.
Retrieve failed: cannot open file | Retrieve was given a name that does not
 exist or cannot be read.
damaged document: a piece of text has no end | The document file is cut or
 damaged.  Use the backup ({n:file}, {n:file-backup}).  The other messages
 about a damaged or too large file are in {x:file-retrieve}.
Import failed: file too large (the limit is 32 MB) | The text file is larger
 than a document can be.  Split it.
Imported only the first part: a document has at most 500000 blocks | The text
 file has more paragraphs than a document can hold; the first ones were
 imported.
.end
.h2 Examples [convert-examples]
.ix Examples, conversion
.ix Conversion examples
Each example gives the input, the steps and the result.
.h3 A table of three rows, in and out [convert-examples-table]
.ix Example, table import and export
A file of three rows, with a Tab between three cells in each:
.code
Item TAB Qty TAB Price
Bolts TAB 12 TAB 3.50
Nuts TAB 100 TAB 0.25
.end
Import with Preserve Line Endings gives one block of three lines with tabs.
Export Data Only writes it back as the same three lines with the tabs, and
a fourth, empty line (the empty line that the end of the last line leaves in the
block).  Export of a screen image, with the default ruler of one stop at 5,
writes
.code
Item Qty Price
Bolts 12 3.50
Nuts 100 0.25
.end
because a tab past the last stop is one cell wide.  With the ruler of the text
set to the stops 12 and 22 the same Export writes
.code
Item        Qty       Price
Bolts       12        3.50
Nuts        100       0.25
.end
which is the table in columns.  The ruler is set by the field Text Ruler of
Global Format, or by the key {k:F6} for one block ({n:format}).
.h3 A text with accents from a modern system [convert-examples-utf8]
.ix Example, UTF-8 text with accents
A letter that was written on a system that uses UTF-8 has the word "caf" with
the e that has the acute accent, and the Spanish word for year, which has the
n with the tilde.  It also has the euro sign.
1. File, Import: the file of the letter, Options Ignore Line Endings, Character
 Set UTF-8.  The accented e and the n with the tilde come in as they are.  The
 euro sign has no place in the document: it is a question mark, and the
 message line shows "Imported; 1 character not kept".
2. Edit, and save the document.  Replace the question mark by the word EUR.
3. To send it back, Export with Export Data Only and Character Set UTF-8.  The
 file has the accented letters as they were.
.h3 A report to a plain text file [convert-examples-text]
.ix Example, document to text
Task: a report that is a structured document is to be read in a mail
program.
1. File, Export: give the name of the text file, set Options to Export a Screen
 Image and the Character Set to UTF-8, and accept with {k:Ins}.  A mail program
 of today reads the file without conversion.
2. Look at the file.  The lines are at most 79 characters long, the headlines
 have their section numbers where the document has numbers, and the columns of
 a block are side by side.
3. For a text that the mail program wraps itself, export with Export Data Only
 instead: the cells of a table are each on a line of their own, the tabs are
 tabs and the blocks have no blank line between them
 ({n:convert-export}).
.h3 A document to a PDF without sending it [convert-examples-pdf]
.ix Example, PDF of a document
A document is to be a PDF for a colleague, with no printer touched.  Use
{k:F12}, Print, Final with the field Printer left empty: the PDF is made with
the settings of the Print panel and the file is named in Output File
({n:print-final}).  When the document is to be typeset again from the TeX
source, Export a LaTeX File
({n:file-export-latex}) writes the source.
